* A batch of 1000 identical TXL (Y card) lines, so the `--backend auto` price
* (src/analysis/gpu/price.zig) puts the batch on the GPU: 1000 instances x 80 KB
* of history price the walk at ~23 ms per iteration on one CPU core against
* ~2.8 ms on the device. This keeps the device's history path in the release
* corpus. Measured on an RTX 4060 Laptop against an i9-14900HX, ReleaseSafe
* (2026-11-02): 3.4 s under --backend cuda against 6.6 s on the CPU, with
* byte-identical output. Narrower is not a win: 500 lines over 713 steps took
* 16.8 s on the device against 10.8 s on the CPU.
* Each line is txl_tran_matched_step.sp's matched line at a tenth of its
* length. Every line sees the same drive, so every v(b<k>) is one line's answer:
* Z0 = sqrt(L/C) = 50 ohm, Td = length*sqrt(L*C) = 0.2 m * 5 ns/m = 1 ns, and
* Rs = RL = Z0 make it a plain delay of v(a<k>) = 0.5 (after the 10 ps edge):
*   t = 0.50 ns  v(a1) = 0.5   v(b<k>) = 0
*   t = 0.95 ns  v(a1) = 0.5   v(b<k>) = 0     (50 ps before arrival)
*   t = 1.20 ns  v(a1) = 0.5   v(b<k>) = 0.5
*   t = 1.40 ns  v(a1) = 0.5   v(b<k>) = 0.5
* Generated: for k in 1..1000: Rs<k> in a<k> 50, Y<k> a<k> 0 b<k> 0 ymod,
* RL<k> b<k> 0 50.
* Expected results: txl_wide_batch_1000.expected.json
